{
  "families": [
    {
      "budget_share": 50,
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 22, "low": 10, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"}
      ],
      "why": "One registered stage per micro-rotation gives 1 result/cycle, so throughput equals Fmax and should comfortably clear 50 MSPS; this is the reference 1/cycle family for the power/accuracy trade-off. W>=12 and N>=10 are needed to approach the 2^-12 error bound, while guards and rounding tune the last bits."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 50,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 22, "low": 10, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 8, "low": 2, "param": "m"}
      ],
      "why": "Registering every m stages keeps 1 result/cycle but cuts pipeline FF count, which is the main lever on power_index; larger m lowers area/power at the cost of a longer combinational path and lower Fmax. Searching m=2..8 maps that power-vs-Fmax frontier and finds the lowest-power points that still hold >=50 MSPS."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The 50 MSPS constraint forces a 1-result-per-cycle architecture, so only the pipelined families are viable; iterative/unrolled_k would need Fmax in the hundreds of MHz. Accuracy <= 2^-12 requires roughly W>=14 and N>=14, so I search W=12..20 and N=10..22 with the full guard/rounding space to let the search find the cheapest point that just meets the error bound. pipelined gives the safe high-Fmax corner, while pipelined_m explores the lower-FF/lower-power corner where the m parameter trades Fmax for power; splitting the budget evenly maps both ends of the Pareto front."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "Feasible designs exist, so `infeasible` is ruled out: 29 of 60 evaluations met both constraints, and the front's weakest-throughput point (pipelined_m dW=19 N=16 A=-1 fg=0 m=3) still delivers 51.9 MSPS >= 50 with max_abs_err 1.02e-4 = 2^-13.26 <= 2^-12, at the lowest power_index seen (2.11). The Pareto front spans power_index 2.11..5.63 against the HV reference of 40 and accuracy_bits 13.3..15.5 against reference 12, i.e. the 'minimise power / trade accuracy' curve is covered from the low-power corner that the selection rule (min power_index) will pick up to the accurate corner (2.17e-5 = 2^-15.5). The coverage limits are explained by the registry, not by a search gap: only pipelined and pipelined_m can hit >=50 MSPS in this cost model (unrolled_k/iterative were not needed to reach the constraint, and pipelined_m's 1 result/cycle is what makes the ~2.1 power_index point reachable at 19-bit/16-iter accuracy), the front is itself spread over data_width 19..20 and both families, and pushing accuracy further only raises power_index monotonically with no new constraint-relevant region behind it. Since this is the final round (budget/round cap reached), and the front is both feasible and covers the accuracy/power trade-off including the selection rule's optimum, I stop with the front as the answer rather than spend a non-existent further round."
}
